<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/documents-review.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/documents-review-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/documents-review.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/indiana-claim-appraisal/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Appraisal Umpire Services</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Neutral Appraisal Umpire Services in Indiana</h1><p class="lede">When two appraisers cannot agree, the umpire decides the items in dispute. We serve as a neutral umpire on Indiana panels, independent of both parties.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Neutral, not an advocate</dd></div><div><dt>Selected by</dt><dd>The two appraisers</dd></div><div><dt>Output</dt><dd>Signed written award</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>The umpire’s job</h2>
<p>Under most appraisal clauses, the two party appraisers select an umpire at the outset or once they reach an impasse. The umpire hears the items still in dispute and decides them. An award signed by the umpire and either appraiser sets the amount of loss.</p>
<h2>How we run an umpire assignment</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Disclosure first.</strong> Before accepting, we disclose any prior relationship with either appraiser, the carrier, the policyholder or counsel.</li>
<li><strong>Clear procedures.</strong> We set out how submissions will be exchanged, whether there will be an inspection and how the hearing will run.</li>
<li><strong>Site inspection</strong> when the disputed items depend on conditions that photos can’t settle.</li>
<li><strong>Itemized decisions.</strong> We decide each disputed item and explain the basis, so the award is understandable.</li>
<li><strong>Award form.</strong> Replacement cost, depreciation and actual cash value are stated separately where the policy calls for it.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What umpires don’t do</h2>
<p>An umpire decides the amount of loss for items in dispute. An umpire doesn’t decide coverage, interpret disputed policy language beyond what valuation requires, or rule on bad-faith or legal claims.</p>
<h2>Requesting an umpire</h2>
<p>Party appraisers, carriers and attorneys can contact us with the names of the parties and a short description of the loss so we can check for conflicts. If we have acted as party appraiser for either side on a related matter, we will decline.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Court appointment</b>If the appraisers can’t agree on an umpire, many policies allow either side to ask a court of record to appoint one. We can be proposed as a candidate in that process.</div>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can you be my appraiser and also the umpire?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. On any panel we serve in only one role. If we are your appraiser, the umpire must be someone else.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who pays the umpire?</summary><div class="a"><p>Most policies split the umpire’s fees and expenses equally between the policyholder and the insurer.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do umpires hold formal hearings?</summary><div class="a"><p>Not always. Many umpire decisions are made on written submissions and an inspection. Larger or more complex disputes may involve a hearing with both appraisers.</p></div></details></div>
